# Output configuration. This is the single source of truth for display layout -
# river has no built-in output config (unlike sway's `output` directive), so
# everything goes through the wlr-output-management protocol, which kanshi,
# wlr-randr and wdisplays are all just clients of.
#
# This replaced a one-shot `wlr-randr --output eDP-1 --scale 1.5` in river/init.
# That line ran once at startup and nothing reapplied it: it could not survive
# hotplug, could not correct drift, and raced against the output being
# advertised. kanshi is a daemon that reapplies the matching profile whenever
# outputs change.
#
# kanshi picks the first profile whose outputs are all present, so `docked` is
# listed before `laptop`.

# Laptop panel + external monitor over HDMI, monitor to the right.
#
# Position is in *logical* pixels, i.e. after scaling. wlroots quantises
# fractional scale to 1/256 steps, and a request of 1.3 lands on 333/256 =
# 1.300781 - so the 2560px panel is 2560 / 1.300781 = 1968 logical pixels wide.
# That is why the external starts at x=1968 and not at 2560.
#
# Worth knowing if this ever looks off by a few pixels: `wlr-randr --scale 1.3`
# rounds the other way, to 332/256 = 1.296875, which would make the panel 1974
# logical pixels wide instead. Trust what `wlr-randr` *reports*, not what was
# requested.
profile docked {
	output eDP-1 mode 2560x1440@120Hz position 0,0 scale 1.3
	output HDMI-A-1 mode 1920x1080@60Hz position 1968,0 scale 1
}

# Laptop panel alone.
profile laptop {
	output eDP-1 mode 2560x1440@120Hz position 0,0 scale 1.3
}
